I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the supplied PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and refine them jointly in GSAS-II. I’ll validate that the exported CIFs, phase fractions, profile, statistics, and `final.gpx` all come from the same final refinement.

The chemistry points first to cobalt tungstate, but this lab can retain precursors or form cobalt oxides/off-stoichiometric intermediates. I’m treating phase count as an inference problem from unmatched peaks, not assuming a single CoWO₄ product. Next I’m extracting peak positions and checking the frozen COD search/index tools before refinement.

The strongest line families separate cleanly into monoclinic WO₃ (the 23.18/23.67/24.42° triplet and its higher-angle repeats) plus wolframite-type CoWO₄ (notably ~19.09, 30.71, 36.44, and 54.18°). CoO and Co₃O₄ are being tested as possible minor phases, but their diagnostic lines are not yet consistently present. I’m now building a quantitative joint refinement so residual peaks—not chemistry alone—decide whether either oxide is retained.

A first two-phase refinement gives about 30 wt% CoWO₄ and 70 wt% WO₃, but the unconstrained profile fit wandered into negative broadening parameters, so that number is provisional. I’ve imposed the laboratory’s physical profile bounds and am comparing several wolframite framework analogues; this should distinguish genuine residual structure error from a spurious minor phase.

The physically bounded trials are still refining. One distant starting analogue (FeWO₄ geometry) diverged at the initial scale step, while the closer Mg/Zn/Mn frameworks continue; I’ll use the converged trials only and restart any promising failed model from the refined cell rather than interpreting a numerical failure as phase evidence.

The candidate search now favors a two-phase model: monoclinic WO₃ (COD 1528915 framework) and wolframite CoWO₄. Alternative monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ models fit worse, and CoO/Co₃O₄ scales collapse to zero rather than explaining the residuals. The remaining misfit is dominated by peak-position/intensity details within the two accepted phases, so I’m refining specimen displacement and the heavy-atom coordinates next.

The calibration variables are nearly degenerate (zero, specimen height, and unit-cell scale correlate above 99%), so I’m not assigning physical meaning to them individually. I’m keeping only the combined peak-position correction that improves the profile, while the current bounded profile pass settles the broadening parameters.

I’m screening the phase-wise isotropic displacement factors because the residual changes systematically with angle. The first batch briefly saturated the local process limit; no scientific files were affected, and I’m letting those short zero-cycle calculations finish before collecting the grid.

The two-phase model is now at the final consistency stage. I’m selecting the last bounded profile setting, checking the remaining instrument-polarization sensitivity, then I’ll freeze and replay the exact GSAS-II project before exporting the deliverables.

The last profile grid settles at a physically allowed low Lorentzian broadening (`X ≈ 0.4`) and improves the fit without introducing strain or negative widths. I’m now checking whether high-angle intensity falloff is better explained by the instrument polarization factor; this directly tests whether the phase fractions are being biased by an arbitrary fixed intensity correction.

The surface-roughness test does not support a roughness correction: allowing the Suortti parameters makes them nearly singular with scale and drives them outside their physical ranges. I’m retaining the simpler no-roughness model, with polarization at the allowed upper edge, and doing one final lattice/zero refinement to ensure peak positions remain consistent after the bounded profile updates.

A geometry audit caught an important issue before export: unconstrained coordinate refinement had moved several oxygen sites by ~0.3–0.7 Å and created implausibly short O–O contacts. That is not defensible for this data or for the stated reference-mismatch scale. I’m rolling those sites back to the parent frameworks and re-refining only within tight, physically motivated coordinate windows; the final fit will be slightly less aggressive but the submitted CIFs will remain chemically meaningful.

The constrained refinement now reproduces the pattern at `Rwp ≈ 6.17%` while keeping every refined coordinate within about 0.008 fractional units of its reference framework. The phase inventory remains unchanged (CoWO₄ + residual WO₃); I’m freezing this model, exporting directly from it, and then replaying the saved project independently against the original 8,301-point pattern.

The independent replay succeeded: it regenerated exactly the submitted curve (`max |Δcalc| ≈ 5×10⁻¹¹ counts`), reproduced 29.141956% CoWO₄ / 70.858044% WO₃, and reported no profile-review flags. I’m doing the final artifact/schema and frozen-flag checks now.

Completed the PXRD analysis.

- CoWO₄ (`P 2/c`): **29.14 wt%**
- Monoclinic WO₃ (`P 21/n`): **70.86 wt%**
- Fit: **Rwp 6.168%**, **Rp 4.544%**, **GOF 1.882**

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. Fine oxygen coordinates remain provisional; fraction uncertainty is conservatively ±2–3 wt% absolute. Independent GSAS-II replay reproduced the profile and fractions exactly with no validation flags.

